Here is the function with two valid inputs.
```javascript
function total(values) {
  if (!Array.isArray(values) || values.some(v =>
    typeof v !== "number" || !Number.isFinite(v) || v < 0
  )) {
    throw new TypeError("Expected finite nonnegative numbers in an array");
  }
  return values.reduce((sum, v) => sum + v, 0);
}
console.log(total([4, 6, 0]));
console.log(total([]));
```
Output:
```text
10
0
```
`reduce` adds each number to the running sum. The starting sum is zero, so an empty list also returns zero.

